w: 1.~Great is the Lord, and great- ly he is to be prais- èd still, With- in the ci- ty of our God, up- on his ho- ly hill.
w: 2.~Mount Zi- on stands most beau- ti- ful, the joy of all the land; The ci- ty of the might- y King on her north side doth stand.
w: 3.~The Lord with- in her pa- la- ces is for a re- fuge known. For, lo, the kings that ga- thered were to- ge- ther, by have gone.
w: 4.~But when they did be- hold the same, they, won- d'ring, would not stay; But, be- ing troub- led at the sight, they thence did haste a- way.
w: 5.~Great ter- ror there took hold on them; they were po- ssessed with fear; Their grief came like a wo- man's pain, when she a child doth bear.
w: 6.~Thou Tar- shish ships with east wind break'st: As we have heard it told, So, in the ci- ty of the Lord, our eyes did it be- hold;
w: 7.~In our God's ci- ty, which his hand for ev- er sta- blish will. We of thy lo- ving\-- kind- ness thought, Lord, in thy tem- ple still.
w: 8.~O Lord, ac- cor- ding to thy name, through all the earth's thy praise; And thy right hand, O Lord, is full of right- eous- ness al- ways.
w: 9.~Be- cause thy judg- ments are made known, let Zi- on mount re- joice; Of Ju- dah let the daugh- ters all send forth a cheer- ful voice.
w: 10.~Walk a- bout Zi- on, and go round; the high tow'rs there- of tell: Con- si- der ye her pa- la- ces, and mark her bul- warks well;
w: 11.~That ye may tell po- ste- ri- ty. For this God doth a- bide Our God for ev- er- more; he will ev'n un- to death us guide.
